# 17. Recommended Technical Architecture

Build this as a native Windows desktop application.

### Suggested technology stack

| Component            | Recommended technology            |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Desktop framework    | C# / .NET 10                      |
| Desktop UI           | WPF or WinUI 3                    |
| Database             | SQLite                            |
| 3D terrain rendering | OpenTK or compatible 3D engine    |
| 2D mapping           | MapLibre or equivalent            |
| GIS processing       | GDAL / OGR                        |
| LiDAR processing     | PDAL                              |
| Elevation processing | GDAL and custom processing tools  |
| AI integration       | OpenAI API                        |
| Image generation     | Image generation API              |
| Mapping              | OpenStreetMap-compatible services |
| Image editing        | SkiaSharp                         |
| Project storage      | Local Windows filesystem          |

These are proposed technologies, not mandatory dependencies. Select stable libraries with compatible Windows builds.

## 17.1 Local database

Use SQLite to store:

- Projects.
- Course information.
- Hole information.
- Routing coordinates.
- Feature locations.
- Terrain metadata.
- Design settings.
- AI prompts.
- Generated image references.
- Construction tasks.
- Playtesting records.
- Version history.

Large source files, LiDAR datasets and generated imagery should be stored in organised project folders, with their file paths and metadata stored in SQLite.

## 17.2 AI integration

Provide an API configuration screen.

Allow users to:

- Enter their API key.
- Select the AI model.
- Configure image generation.
- Set image resolution.
- Manage generation history.
- Review API usage.
- Configure generation limits.

Store API keys securely using Windows Credential Manager or Windows DPAPI, never directly in source code or the SQLite database.

AI features should fail gracefully when internet access or API services are unavailable.

## 17.3 GIS and LiDAR architecture

Keep GIS and LiDAR processing in separate modules.

Use background processing so large datasets do not freeze the main application.

Include:

- Processing progress indicators.
- Cancellation.
- Error logging.
- Automatic temporary-file cleanup.
- Memory usage monitoring.
- Processing history.

Support large LiDAR files through tiled or chunked processing rather than loading entire datasets into memory.

## 17.4 Data backup

All course projects must remain accessible offline.

Include:

- Automatic project backups.
- Manual backups.
- Project duplication.
- ZIP project archives.
- Restore functionality.
- Database integrity checks.

Internet connectivity should only be required for external services such as AI generation, map imagery and online geographical data.

